[{"id":"confusing-words-1","question":"Choose the most suitable option to replace the highlighted part of the sentence:<br><br>The man was **effected by** the remarks made by his peers.<br><br>","options":["affected with","effected with","affected by","effected to"],"answer":2,"explanation":"\"Affected by\" is the correct phrase meaning influenced by. \"Effected\" means brought about, so it is incorrect here.","source":"+2 / -0.5","tag":"SSC CHSL, 19 Nov 2025, Shift 3"},{"id":"confusing-words-2","question":"Choose the most suitable option to replace the highlighted part of the sentence:<br>The **dessert** is a dry piece of land.<br><br><br><br><br><br>","options":["desert","dissert","daisert","desertive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct word for a dry piece of land is \"desert\". \"Dessert\" means a sweet course after a meal. The other options are incorrect spellings or unrelated forms.","source":"+2 / -0.5","tag":"SSC CHSL, 21 Nov 2025, Shift 2"},{"id":"confusing-words-3","question":"Choose the correct word.<br><br>She decided to ______ down and rest for a while.<br><br><br><br><br><br>","options":["lie","lye","lay","lei"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct phrase is **lie down**, where **lie** means to recline. **Lay** usually takes an object, as in “lay the book down.”","source":"+2 / -0.5","tag":"SSC CHSL, 24 Nov 2025, Shift 3"},{"id":"confusing-words-4","question":"Choose the sentence with correct usage of \"less\" and \"fewer\".<br><br><br><br><br><br>","options":["She has less books than I do.","There are fewer sugar in the jar.","We need fewer volunteers this time.","He has fewer money than his brother."],"answer":2,"explanation":"\"Fewer\" is used with countable nouns, and \"volunteers\" is countable. The other options incorrectly use \"less\" or \"fewer\" with the wrong noun type.","source":"+2 / -0.5","tag":"SSC CHSL, 26 Nov 2025, Shift 3"},{"id":"confusing-words-5","question":"Which sentence correctly uses confirm?<br><br><br><br><br><br>","options":["Please confirm your attendance before Friday.","They conform the package before delivery.","He couldn’t confirm to the crowd's pressure.","She confirmed with the regulations strictly."],"answer":0,"explanation":"\"Confirm\" means verify or state with assurance. Only option (a) uses it correctly. Option (b) confuses it with \"conform.\"","source":"+1 / -0.25","tag":"SSC CPO, 11 Dec 2025, Shift 1"},{"id":"confusing-words-6","question":"Which sentence uses \"borne\" correctly?<br><br><br><br><br><br>","options":["The cost was borne by the company.","She was borne in 2001.","He has borne arms for years.","The queen has born two sons."],"answer":0,"explanation":"\"Borne\" is the past participle of \"bear\" in the sense of carried or endured. Thus \"The cost was borne by the company\" is correct. \"Born\" is used for birth.","source":"+2 / -0.25","tag":"SSC CPO, 12 Dec 2025, Shift 1"},{"id":"confusing-words-7","question":"<p><b>Choose the option that will replace the error highlighted in bold. Please advise on how I can give my advise .</b></p>","options":["advise, advice","advice, advise","advise, advise","advice, advice"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Correct option (a) <b>advise, advice</b>","source":"+2.0 / -0.5","tag":"Selection Post Phase XIV (10th), 01 Aug 2025, Shift 1"},{"id":"confusing-words-8","question":"<p><b>Choose the option that will replace the error highlighted in bold. Despite his apology, she refused to accept his gift and did not except his explanation.</b></p>","options":["accept, accept","except, accept","accept, except","except, except"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Correct option (a) <b>accept, accept</b>","source":"+2.0 / -0.5","tag":"Selection Post Phase XIV (10th), 01 Aug 2025, Shift 3"},{"id":"confusing-words-9","question":"Choose the correct word to complete the sentence.<br>\nHe will ___ the award at the ceremony tomorrow.","options":["<span style=\"color: rgb(88, 89, 91);\">except</span>","<span style=\"color: rgb(88, 89, 91);\">accept</span>","<span style=\"color: rgb(88, 89, 91);\">access</span>","<span style=\"color: rgb(88, 89, 91);\">aspect</span>"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Correct option (b)<br><br>\n<div><span style=\"\">The correct answer is '</span><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\"><strong>accept</strong>'</span><span style=\"\">.</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span style=\"\"><span style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center;\"><imgpx\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"px\" / style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;object-fit:contain;\" loading=\"lazy\"><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 21px;\"><u>Key Points</u></span></strong></span></span></div>\n<ul>\n    <li><span style=\"\">The word \"<strong>accept</strong>\" means to agree to receive or take something, which fits the context of receiving the award.</span></li>\n    <li><span style=\"\">The word \"<strong>except</strong>\" means to exclude and does not fit the sentence contextually.</span></li>\n    <li><span style=\"\">\"<strong>Access</strong>\" refers to the ability to approach or enter a place, which is unrelated to receiving an award.</span></li>\n    <li><span style=\"\">\"<strong>Aspect</strong>\" means a feature or part of something and is not relevant to the given sentence.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<div><span style=\"\">Therefore, the correct answer is <strong>Option 2</strong>.</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span style=\"\"><strong>Complete Sentence:</strong> <em>\"He will <u><strong>accept</strong></u> the award at the ceremony tomorrow.\"</em></span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span style=\"\"><span style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center;\"><imgpx\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"px\" / style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;object-fit:contain;\" loading=\"lazy\"><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></span></div>\n<ul>\n    <li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 1: \"except\"</strong> is incorrect because it means to exclude and does not align with the context of receiving an award.</span></li>\n    <li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 3: \"access\"</strong> is incorrect because it refers to entering or approaching a place and has no relevance here.</span></li>\n    <li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 4: \"aspect\"</strong> is incorrect because it refers to a feature or part of something, which is unrelated to the context.</span></li>\n</ul>","source":"+1 / -0.25","tag":""},{"id":"confusing-words-10","question":"Choose the correct word to complete the sentence.<br>\nHe was asked to ________ the meeting.","options":["precede","proceed","preseed","process"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Correct option (b)<br><br><div><span style=\"\">The correct answer is '</span><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\"><strong>proceed</strong>'</span><span style=\"\">.</span></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><span style=\"\"><span style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center;\"><imgpx\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"px\" / style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;object-fit:contain;\" loading=\"lazy\"><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 21px;\"><u>Key Points</u></span></strong></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><span style=\"\">The word <strong>\"proceed\"</strong> means to continue or begin an action, especially after a pause or interruption.</span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"\">In the context of the sentence, <strong>\"He was asked to proceed the meeting\"</strong>, it is grammatically correct as it refers to starting or continuing the meeting.</span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"\">Options <strong>1, 3,</strong> and <strong>4</strong> are incorrect because:</span>\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 1: \"precede\"</strong> means to come before something, which does not fit the context.</span></li>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 3: \"preseed\"</strong> is not a valid word in English.</span></li>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 4: \"process\"</strong> refers to a series of steps to achieve a result, which does not fit the meaning intended in the sentence.</span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div><span style=\"\">Therefore, the correct answer is <strong>Option 2</strong>.</span></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><span style=\"\"><strong>Complete Sentence:</strong> <em>\"He was asked to <u><strong>proceed</strong></u> the meeting.\"</em></span></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><span style=\"\"><span style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center;\"><imgpx\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"px\" / style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;object-fit:contain;\" loading=\"lazy\"><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle; font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 1: \"precede\"</strong> is incorrect because it means to come before something in time or order, which does not align with the context.</span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 3: \"preseed\"</strong> is invalid as it is not a recognized English word.</span></li>\n\t<li><span style=\"\"><strong>Option 4: \"process\"</strong> is incorrect because it refers to a series of steps or actions, not the act of continuing or starting a meeting.</span></li>\n</ul>","source":"+1 / -0.25","tag":""},{"id":"confusing-words-11","question":"<span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">Which word matches the clue \"to praise\"?</span>","options":["<span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">Compliment</span>","<span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">Complement</span>","<span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">Complimentary</span>","<span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">Complyment</span>"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Correct option (a)<br><br><p>The correct answer is: <b>Option 1 i.e. Compliment</b>.</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><imgpx\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"px\" / style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;object-fit:contain;\" loading=\"lazy\"><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Key Points</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>The word <strong>\"Compliment\"</strong> means to express praise, admiration, or approval for someone or something. (प्रशंसा करना)\n\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><strong>Example: </strong>She received a lot of compliments on her beautiful dress.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>The correct word here is <strong>\"Compliment\"</strong> because the question asks for a word that matches the clue \"to praise\". (प्रशंसा करना)\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><strong>Example: </strong>He complimented the chef for the delicious meal.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is: <strong>Option 1 i.e. Compliment</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><imgpx\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"px\" / style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:8px 0;border-radius:4px;object-fit:contain;\" loading=\"lazy\"><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></p>\n\n<p>Here are the other options explained along with their Hindi meanings and example sentences:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li><strong>Complement</strong> (पूरक): Something that completes or goes well with something else.\n\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><strong>Example:</strong> The blue curtains complement the yellow walls beautifully.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li><strong>Complimentary</strong> (निःशुल्क): Something given for free or expressing praise.\n\t<ul>\n\t\t<li><strong>Example:</strong> The hotel offered complimentary breakfast to all its guests.</li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li><strong>Complyment</strong> (invalid word): This is a misspelling and does not exist in English vocabulary.</li>\n</ul>","source":"+1 / -0.25","tag":""},{"id":"confusing-words-12","question":"**Choose the correct word to complete the sentence.**<br><br>The writer often used clever ______ to hint at deeper meanings.","options":["allusions","illusions","elusions","delusions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Allusions” are indirect references that hint at something (literature, history, ideas), fitting the context of deeper meanings. “Illusions” are false perceptions; “delusions” are fixed false beliefs; “elusions” is not the intended word here (rare/incorrect in this context). Learning points: allusion ≠ illusion (reference vs false appearance); “to allude to” = to refer indirectly; writers use allusions to add layers of meaning; context clue “hint” strongly signals “allusions”; build vocabulary by common pairs (affect/effect, allusion/illusion).","source":"+3 / -1","tag":"SSC MTS, 09 Feb 2025, Shift 1"},{"id":"confusing-words-13","question":"**Choose the correct word to complete the sentence.**<br><br>The thief tried to ______ the police by hiding in the forest.","options":["elude","allude","illusion","allusion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Elude” means to evade/escape capture, which fits the context of avoiding the police. “Allude” means to refer indirectly; “illusion” is a false perception; “allusion” is an indirect reference. Learning points: (1) Elude (verb) = evade. (2) Allude (verb) = hint/mention indirectly. (3) Allusion (noun) = indirect reference. (4) Illusion (noun) = deceptive appearance. (5) In vocabulary questions, identify part of speech needed by the sentence.","source":"+3 / -1","tag":"SSC MTS, 11 Feb 2025, Shift 2"},{"id":"confusing-words-14","question":"<br>Which sentence has the correct usage?","options":["He is a man of high council.","He is a man of high counsel.","He is a man of high cancel.","He is a man of high consul."],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Counsel” means advice or a legal adviser; “a man of high counsel” implies a person of great wisdom/advice. “Council” is an assembly/committee; “cancel” is a verb; “consul” is a diplomatic officer. Learning points: council (group) vs counsel (advice/lawyer); sound-alike errors are common; check part of speech; meaning must fit the sentence.","source":"+3 / -1","tag":"SSC MTS, 12 Feb 2025, Shift 2"},{"id":"confusing-words-15","question":"**Choose the correct word to complete the sentence.**<br><br>The school will _____ the new rules from next Monday.","options":["adept","adopt","addict","adapt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Adopt” means to accept and start using/implementing something (rules/policy), so it is correct. “Adept” is an adjective meaning skilled. “Addict” is generally a noun/verb related to dependency. “Adapt” means to adjust/modify to suit conditions, not to formally implement rules. Learning points: (1) adopt = accept/implement; (2) adapt = adjust/change; (3) adept = skilled; (4) common exam trap: adopt vs adapt; (5) collocation: “adopt rules/policy/measure”.","source":"+3 / -1","tag":"SSC MTS, 13 Feb 2025, Shift 2"},{"id":"confusing-words-16","question":"<br>Choose the correct word to complete the sentence.<br><br>The teacher advised him to _____ his mistakes instead of hiding them.","options":["admit","admet","omit","admin"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Admit” means to acknowledge or confess, which fits the context: admit mistakes. “Admet” is incorrect spelling. “Omit” means leave out/skip, which doesn’t fit. “Admin” is a noun/short form and not appropriate here.<br>Learning points: (1) Correct collocation: admit a mistake. (2) Grammar: “to + base verb” → “to admit.” (3) Avoid near-looking words: omit vs admit. (4) Spelling trap: admet. (5) Meaning-based completion is safer than guessing.","source":"+3 / -1","tag":"SSC MTS, 17 Feb 2025, Shift 1"}]